---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import DataTable from '../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion, nodes, edges } from '../lib/feeds'

const nine = assertion('nine-offices-eleven-officers')
const appointed = assertion('appointment-is-a-form-of-local-government')
const twoVillages = assertion('two-villages-cannot-fill-their-governments')
const townships = assertion('the-townships-are-named')
const limaWards = assertion('lima-elects-by-ward-and-fills-its-seats')
const mayors = assertion('the-mayoral-line-stops-in-1886')

// Built from the graph rather than typed out, so the page cannot drift from the corpus.
// An office with no tenure would show as a blank holder rather than silently vanish.
const byId = new Map(nodes.map((n) => [n.id, n]))
const offices = nodes
  .filter((n) => n.class === 'office' && n.properties.office_type === 'elected')
  .filter((n) => n.id.includes('allen-county'))
  .toSorted((a, b) => a.label.localeCompare(b.label))

const holdersOf = (officeId: string) =>
  edges
    .filter((e) => e.relationship === 'of-office' && e.to === officeId)
    .map((e) => byId.get(e.from))
    .filter((t): t is NonNullable<typeof t> => Boolean(t))
    .map((tenure) => {
      const held = edges.find((e) => e.relationship === 'held-by' && e.from === tenure.id)
      return {
        person: (held && byId.get(held.to)?.label) ?? '—',
        began: tenure.properties.began ?? '',
        ended: tenure.properties.ended ?? '',
        label: tenure.label,
      }
    })
    .toSorted((a, b) => b.began.localeCompare(a.began))

// A current holder is one whose tenure has no `ended` — not the one with the latest start.
// Sorting by start date put the wrong sheriff here: Everett and Treglia both began in 2017,
// because Everett's term ended in the year Treglia's began.
const currentOf = (officeId: string) =>
  holdersOf(officeId).filter((h) => !h.ended)

const rows = offices.flatMap((o) => {
  const held = currentOf(o.id).toSorted((a, b) => a.began.localeCompare(b.began))
  return held.map((h, i) => [
    i === 0 ? o.label : '',
    h.person,
    h.began,
    i === 0 ? (o.properties.term_length ?? '—') : '',
  ])
})

const courts = nodes.filter((n) => n.properties.jurisdiction_type === 'court district')
const judicial = nodes
  .filter((n) => n.class === 'office' && n.properties.office_type === 'judicial')
  .toSorted((a, b) => a.label.localeCompare(b.label))
const judgeRows = judicial.flatMap((o) =>
  currentOf(o.id)
    .toSorted((a, b) => a.began.localeCompare(b.began))
    .map((h, i) => [i === 0 ? o.label : '', h.person, h.began]),
)

// Representation: the offices whose constituency is a district rather than the county. They are
// the ones that point at a division, or at the appellate court that is not the county's own.
const REP = [
  'office/member-of-the-ohio-house-for-district-78.yml',
  'office/member-of-the-ohio-senate-for-district-12.yml',
  'office/representative-in-congress-for-ohios-4th-district.yml',
]
const repRows = REP.flatMap((id) => {
  const o = byId.get(id)
  if (!o) throw new Error(`no office ${id} in the feed`)
  return currentOf(id).map((h) => [o.label, h.person, h.began])
})

const tenureCounts = offices.map((o) => [o.label, holdersOf(o.id).length])
// An office is "traced" when the corpus holds more tenures than the office has seats — that
// is, at least one predecessor. Three commissioners in three seats is not a line of holders.
const traced = offices.filter(
  (o) => holdersOf(o.id).length > Number(o.properties.seats ?? '1'),
).length
---

<Base
  title="Government"
  lede="The nine elected offices that govern Allen County, the two hundred and eleven people who hold elective office over its ground, and how far back this corpus can see."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Nine offices, eleven officers</h2>
    <p>
      Ohio counties are not home-rule corporations. They are administrative subdivisions of the
      state, and the state fixes what offices they have: a board of three commissioners elected
      countywide, and eight other officers elected separately. Allen County has all nine and no
      more, and nothing on this page distinguishes it from the other eighty-seven Ohio counties
      except the names.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={nine} />

    <DataTable
      title="The elected offices of Allen County"
      caption="Holders and term commencement dates from the Ohio boards of elections' county lookup, read 30 August 2026. A term start is not a start of service: six of these officers may have held office longer, and this corpus does not know."
      columns={['Office', 'Holder', 'Term began', 'Term']}
      rows={rows}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Three seats, three days apart</h2>
    <p>
      The board's three commissioners hold terms that begin on the first, second and third day of
      January. That looks like a clerical artifact and is not one: Ohio law assigns those three
      dates precisely so the seats stay distinguishable and the board never turns over at once.
      One seat is elected two years out of step with the other two.
    </p>
    <p>
      The auditor is the only county officer whose term does not begin in January. It begins on the
      second Monday in March — 13 March 2023 for the present term, which was that Monday. Both
      rules were read out of the statute <em>after</em> the dates had been read off the elections
      roster, and each predicted what was already in hand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Two offices you must be qualified to hold</h2>
    <p>
      Most of these offices ask only that the holder be an elector of the county. Two ask more. The
      coroner must be a physician licensed to practise medicine or osteopathic medicine and in good
      standing, and must have held that licence for two years before election — which in a county of
      about a hundred thousand people makes the candidate pool a profession rather than an
      electorate. The engineer must be <em>both</em> a registered professional engineer and a
      registered surveyor.
    </p>
    <p>
      The engineer's statute also disqualifies four of the county's other elected officers by name:
      the clerk of courts, the sheriff, the treasurer and the recorder may not hold it. Every one of
      those four is a node in this corpus, which is what makes that clause a constraint on the graph
      and not just a sentence in a code book.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How far back this page can see</h2>
    <p>
      Not far, and unevenly. Of the nine offices, {traced} holds any predecessor at all — the
      sheriff, whose own office publishes a roster back to 1831. The other eight have the present
      term and nothing before it, because the source that names them is a roster of who holds
      office now.
    </p>
    <p>
      That asymmetry is the reason this page exists at all. For nine phases this corpus held two
      offices — the sheriff, with thirty-nine tenures back to 1831, and the mayor of Lima — and the
      depth of the first made the class look worked. It was one office in extraordinary detail and
      eight missing entirely.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      title="Tenures held per office"
      caption="What the corpus has read, not what the county has had. A count of one means the present term and no predecessor."
      columns={['Office', 'Tenures in the corpus']}
      rows={tenureCounts}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The courts</h2>
    <p>
      {courts.length} courts sit over this ground and none of them is part of the county government. The
      Court of Common Pleas is the county's trial court, divided five ways by its own rules and
      staffed by four elected judges. The Third District Court of Appeals hears appeals from
      seventeen Ohio counties and keeps its office at 204 N. Main Street in Lima, so a court
      serving a sixth of the state is seated in this county and its records are kept by this
      county's clerk.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the Lima Municipal Court's territory is not Lima. Ohio's statute gives the general rule
      that a municipal court has jurisdiction "within the corporate limits of their respective
      municipal corporations", then names this one as an exception: <em>"The Lima municipal court
      has jurisdiction within Allen county."</em> A traffic case from Spencerville is heard by a
      court named after a city thirteen miles away.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      title="Judges elected by Allen County's voters"
      caption="Six judges across two courts. Every judicial office in this corpus runs six years and every elected administrative one runs four — computed from one county's roster, so a pattern in the data rather than a rule read anywhere."
      columns={['Court and division', 'Judge', 'Term began']}
      rows={judgeRows}
    />

    <p>
      The Third District's four judges sit on that table too, and did not last week. They are
      elected by the voters of all seventeen counties, and a rule written one phase ago excluded
      them on that ground. The rule's next case was this county's own state senator and state
      representative, elected by multi-county districts, which it excluded as well — so the rule
      went. This corpus now holds a person wherever the constituency is smaller than the state and
      includes ground in Allen County.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Who represents the county</h2>
    <p>
      One member of the Ohio House, one of the Ohio Senate, one of Congress. The county is split
      between districts in neither state chamber — the roster names one of each, and a split county
      would name two.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      title="Allen County's representation"
      caption="From the Ohio boards of elections' county lookup, read 30 August 2026. The House seat runs two years, the Senate seat four, and the congressional seat two — the shortest terms in this corpus, against four years for a county officer and six for a judge."
      columns={['Seat', 'Holder', 'Term began']}
      rows={repRows}
    />

    <p>
      The state legislative districts are current: Ohio's legislative map was adopted on 29 September
      2023 and is the map still in force, the one the November 2024 election ran on. The county was
      House District 4 under the previous map — a district coterminous with the county, to the byte
      — and is the 78th now.
    </p>
    <p>
      The congressional seat is a different matter, and it is the reason this page will not tell you
      which congressional district Allen County is in. Ohio adopted a new congressional map on
      31 October 2025. The county's sitting representative was elected in November 2024, under the
      map that one replaced. So the roster establishes who represents this county and not which
      district the county is now in, and the corpus has an open question that says so rather than
      closing the gap with a guess. One roster, one election, two vintages.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The government below the county</h2>
    <p>
      Nine offices is what the state gives a county. It is not what governs this ground. Twelve
      townships, seven villages, two cities, nine school boards and an educational service centre
      sit under the county, and between them they fill a hundred and seventy-one elective seats to
      the county's twenty-two.
    </p>
    <p>
      The roster this page is built from is indexed by voting precinct, not by county: each page
      lists the offices its own precinct votes on and no others. For nine phases this corpus read
      one of those pages and took it for the roster. Reading all eighty-eight gives the whole of
      it — a hundred and one offices, two hundred and eighteen seats, seven of them empty.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={townships} />
    <p>
      Each township elects two trustees in one odd year and the third two years later, and its
      fiscal officer on a term year that begins on 1 April where every trustee's begins on
      1 January. Nothing this corpus reasoned about the shape of a township board would have
      produced that last detail; it came off the seats.
    </p>

    <h3>Appointment is a form of government here</h3>
    <AssertionCard assertion={appointed} />
    <p>
      It concentrates in one form of government and not in small places generally. Twenty-five of
      the forty-seven seated village officers were appointed; across all forty-eight township seats
      there is one. Spencer Township and Spencerville govern nearly the same people, and the
      township elected all four of its officers while the village appointed four of its seven.
      Jackson Township and the village of Lafayette are run out of the same building at 225 E.
      Sugar Street and are staffed in opposite ways.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={twoVillages} />
    <p>
      Nor is it the smallest places failing. Beaverdam is the smallest village in the county and
      Bluffton the largest, and they are the two that appoint least. Why the village form is
      staffed this way and the township form is not is an open question in this corpus, and it
      stays open: a roster records how a sitting officer arrived, never whether anyone stood. The
      election returns would say, and no village race has been read.
    </p>
  </section>

    <h3>The city</h3>
    <p>
      For eleven phases this corpus could name every sheriff of Allen County since 1831 and one
      officer of the city that holds a third of the county's people. Lima's government is described
      here for the first time: a mayor, an auditor, a director of law, a president of council and
      seven council members, one for each ward.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={limaWards} />
    <p>
      The wards are staggered by parity — first, third, fifth and seventh turned over in January
      2026, second, fourth and sixth in January 2024 — so four seats and three alternate every two
      years and no single election can turn the council over. Nobody on the ballot for any of the
      eleven carries a party letter, where the county and state holders on the same roster do.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the city keeps four term calendars at once. The mayor's year runs 1 December to
      30 November; the auditor's and the law director's 1 January to 31 December; the president's
      and the even wards' 1 January to 2 January; the odd wards' 5 January to 6 January. That is
      what a charter looks like from outside it, and Lima has one — its own site links "Lima City
      Charter and Code" — which this corpus has located and not read, because the code library
      refuses an automated client.
    </p>
    <p>
      The city's first mayor was Henry D. V. Williams, elected when the town organized on 29 March
      1842, eleven years after the county commissioners platted it and while they still ran it. The
      1885 county history prints the mayor of every year between; the corpus holds two of them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What is still missing</h2>
    <p>
      Every holder of these offices before the current term, except the sheriff's. The powers each
      office actually exercises, as against the statute that creates it. The subject-matter
      jurisdiction of both trial courts — the corpus has established their territories and neither's
      competence. And the appointed officials and boards that do much of a county's work without
      appearing on any ballot, which is now the largest block of officeholders this corpus does not
      hold, the townships having been named.
    </p>
    <p>
      Delphos is the one government on this page that is knowably incomplete. Its ward council seats
      are voted on inside their wards, and 3,182 of the city's people live in Van Wert County, so an
      Allen County roster cannot see any ward west of the line. Ten of its seats appear here and
      they are not the whole of its council.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The mayors, and the gap after them</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={mayors} />
    <p>
      The 1885 county history narrates Lima's municipal elections year by year from the town's
      organization on 29 March 1842 and then prints a list of mayors to 1886. That is twenty-four
      men over twenty-seven terms in forty-four years. This site had said "about forty holders in
      forty-three years" for twelve phases, written from the shape of the passage rather than from
      counting it, and both halves were wrong.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Mayors of Lima, 1842–1886</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Years</th><th scope="col">Mayor</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1842–44</td><td>Henry D. V. Williams</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1844–45</td><td>H. F. Hubbard</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1845–46</td><td>John P. Fay</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1846–47</td><td>Thomas Delzell</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1847–48</td><td>Aurelius N. Smith</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1848–49</td><td>James Cunningham</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1849–50</td><td>Thomas Delzell</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1850–51</td><td>Thomas Milligan</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1851–53</td><td>Samuel A. Baxter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1853–54</td><td>Thomas Milligan</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1854–56</td><td>Samuel A. Baxter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1856–57</td><td>James E. Harriott</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1857–58</td><td>John Melhorn</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1858–59</td><td>James A. Newell</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1859–60</td><td>John L. Hughes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1860–61</td><td>E. G. Hamilton</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1861–64</td><td>John R. Hughes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1864–65</td><td>J. T. Heckman</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1865–68</td><td>John Collett</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1868–69</td><td>Thomas M. Robb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1869–70</td><td>George W. Overmyer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1870–74</td><td>Richard Metheany</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1874–76</td><td>John C. Dunlevy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1876–80</td><td>John B. Townsend</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1880–82</td><td>Frank E. Mead</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1882–84</td><td>H. S. Prophet</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1884–86</td><td>William McComb</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Four of the twenty-four have pages of their own here and twenty do not, which is a decision
      rather than an unfinished job: a holder earns a node when something about that holder does not
      fit the line. Samuel A. Baxter was mayor twice and appointed once between, and is probably the
      Dr. Samuel A. Baxter who co-wrote the 1906 county history — which would mean the technical
      account of the first oil well that this site has leaned on was written partly by a man who had
      been Lima's mayor thirty-four years before it was drilled. George W. Overmyer is the only
      mayor in the line no election produced; the council chose him in September 1869 when Robb
      resigned, and he was probate judge in 1881 on the committee that picked the plans for the
      present courthouse. Richard Metheany appears in the table six times across twenty-six years.
      And two sources spell H. S. Prophet differently.
    </p>
    <p>
      The book prints the whole council beside the mayor, and that is where the rest of this site
      turns up. Benjamin C. Faurot was elected a village trustee in 1867, eighteen years before the
      well that made him. Matthias H. Nichols was a trustee in 1849, after three terms in Congress
      and twelve years before he raised the county's first company for the war. Thomas K. Jacobs was
      trustee, marshal and treasurer, and introduced the bill in the Ohio House in April 1861 to tax
      the county for soldiers' families.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
